  "notes": "Warren J. Smith, Modern Lens Design, 2nd ed., Figure 21.14, p. 409. Book: EFL -15990.0, BFL 1024, NA 0.0067 (F/88.5), HFOV 2.53, VL 358.55, infinite conjugate. Wavelengths 10 um as in the book (primary first). Glass: BS1 entered as 2.492 (constant index, chalcogenide glass; the design uses a single wavelength); GERMA entered as 4.003329 (constant index inside the book's rounded 4.003, chosen to match the book's EFL; the design uses a single wavelength). The book does not mark the stop; it is placed at surface 1, the front surface. Infrared materials come from the Zemax INFRARED catalog; the book lists their indices at the primary wavelength from older data, so small differences in EFL are expected. Afocal 16x telescope. As in the book (Afo 1000, MAG 16), an ideal lens of focal length 1000 follows it so it can be evaluated at a finite focus. EPD = |EFL|/F# (180.68). The book's VL 358.55 leaves out the 23.660 air space before the ideal lens. Discrepancy with the book: the telescope is so nearly afocal that the EFL and image distance behind the ideal lens change with the fourth decimal of the germanium index; with germanium at 4.00333 the EFL matches, but the paraxial image lies about 998 behind the ideal lens instead of the book's 1024, so the image is left at the printed 1000.",
